    "language": "Greek",
    "verdict": "EQUIVALENT",
    "winner": "equivalent",
    "fabricated": "none",
    "why": "Left correctly renders the one Italian line about a duplicate copy without staining and with marginal notes; right correctly renders the Latin Jesuit Roman College provenance line and every shelfmark number."
   },
   {
    "id": "69943f85338b1d7fd14de391:1-2",
    "language": "Syriac",
    "verdict": "EQUIVALENT",
    "winner": "equivalent",
    "fabricated": "none",
    "why": "Both are boilerplate pages handled faithfully: left reproduces the cover image description of the marbled binding with no invented text, and right passes through the English Google disclaimer verbatim including the books.google.com URL."
   },
   {
    "id": "69a5e9cb787d6e9b8d42e730:106-105",
    "language": "Armenian",
    "verdict": "EQUIVALENT",
    "winner": "equivalent",
    "fabricated": "none",
    "why": "Left correctly renders the two-line Mesrop Mashtots caption verse and glosses vardapet, while right accurately carries the whole Book Two preface including the David and Solomon citations and the cloud-then-sun, winter-then-spring simile."
   },
   {
    "id": "69e7abf15f1a22ab19aa0c01:244-245",
    "language": "Tibetan",
    "verdict": "EQUIVALENT",
    "winner": "equivalent",
    "fabricated": "none",
    "why": "Both sources are degenerate looping Uchen OCR and both translations track it clause by clause without inventing content - left even annotates that the repeated phrase fills the rest of the page, right mirrors the repeated bsgrub/dge slong clauses."
   },
   {
    "id": "69e010c0c47b5c9f88213cee:17-18",
    "language": "Javanese",
    "verdict": "EQUIVALENT",
    "winner": "equivalent",
    "fabricated": "none",
    "why": "Both pages are looping Javanese-script OCR and both translations mirror the loop without inventing content; left misreads the running head kaca pitung puluh (page seventy) as Book of Seventy-One, right renders wiwitipun dumateng pungkasanipun correctly and flags the repetition as an OCR artefact."
   },
   {
    "id": "69e75cfacc48e59ad74eb323:45-46",
    "language": "Japanese",
    "verdict": "EQUIVALENT",
    "winner": "equivalent",
    "fabricated": "none",
    "why": "Both track their own garbled kuzushiji OCR: left renders the Mutsu/travel imagery actually present in it, and rights repeated ku-gu-ke-fu block mirrors an OCR that itself looped, though its gomaten-notation note is unsupported."
   },
   {
    "id": "69c1bbf98522835be8460ee1:32-33",
    "language": "Hebrew and Judeo-Arabic",
    "verdict": "LEFT_BETTER",
    "winner": "lite",
    "fabricated": "none",
    "why": "Left renders the Syriac page line by line accurately (doxology, the prophet quotation, the Mar Ephrem rubric and death-remembrance verses), while right translates only five lines and replaces the rest with a note speculating that the OCR loop is mnemonic or liturgical repetition."
   },
   {
    "id": "69e013c593b116d24238b3d7:43-44",
    "language": "Egyptian hieroglyphs",
    "verdict": "LEFT_BETTER",
    "winner": "lite",
    "fabricated": "none",
    "why": "Faced with equally degenerate glyph loops, left renders the two real lines (it-netjer, Amun) and flags the repetition in one note, while right mechanically expands the loop into thousands of words of Amun / Priest of Amun / Name of Amun."
   },
   {
    "id": "69c1bb658522835be845f635:126-127",
    "language": "Ladino and French",
    "verdict": "LEFT_BETTER",
    "winner": "lite",
    "fabricated": "none",
    "why": "Left renders the Syriac page clause by clause, including the 1 Cor 1:18 quotation and the Moses / prophets / John the Baptist sequence, while right mechanically expands a degenerate Avestan OCR loop into ~80 identical This word, O Lord Wisdom lines."
   },
   {
    "id": "69c1bc0b8522835be84613b7:69-70",
    "language": "Hebrew",
    "verdict": "EQUIVALENT",
    "winner": "equivalent",
    "fabricated": "none",
    "why": "Both sides face degenerate Syriac OCR and neither invents: left renders the paragraph-35 opening on the soul in the body and explicitly flags the hundred-fold repeated clause it skips, while right renders its varying power/region/discernment loop in full, adding only an unsourced Discernment heading."
   },
   {
    "id": "69c1baf98522835be845be90:32-33",
    "language": "Hebrew, Aramaic, and Judeo-Arabic",
    "verdict": "EQUIVALENT",
    "winner": "equivalent",
    "fabricated": "none",
    "why": "Left OCR is a pure degenerate loop with no body text and left renders it honestly with a warning instead of inventing verses, while right accurately renders its real Leviticus 9 Hebrew-plus-Targum text verse by verse."
   },
   {
    "id": "69c1bd498522835be8468c64:26-27",
    "language": "Geez",
    "verdict": "EQUIVALENT",
    "winner": "equivalent",
    "fabricated": "none",
    "why": "Left OCR is one clause looped to 18K chars and left mirrors the loop without inventing content, while right faithfully renders its genuine Stephen-protomartyr liturgy line by line including the Acts 7 phrases (I saw heaven opened, forgive them)."
   },
   {
    "id": "69c1bb688522835be845f7d9:329-330",
    "language": "Judeo-Arabic and Hebrew",
    "verdict": "RIGHT_BETTER",
    "winner": "lite",
    "fabricated": "left",
    "why": "Right renders every table cell of the Hebrew word-square exactly (Joseph, Abraham, Shaddai, the Prophet), while left translates an all-unclear grid by adding unsupported editorial matter — a previous-page Syriac colophon and a Bahra Hassab computus identification found nowhere in its OCR."
   },
   {
    "id": "69e8b2a72ff2a8dc09e7860d:25-66",
    "language": "Arabic",
    "verdict": "EQUIVALENT",
    "winner": "equivalent",
    "fabricated": "none",
    "why": "Both render their Ihya pages complete from first clause to the closing footnote with accurate technical terms, and right's added Quran citations (49:10, 43:67, 11:29) and left's hadith-chain footnote are both correct."
   },
   {
    "id": "69b9a0fe4a1112994cb81c4d:198-199",
